---
import { storyEyebrow, storyTitle } from '../data/stories';
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import StoryFrame from '../components/StoryFrame.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';

const APP = 'https://atheer-next.fly.dev/';
const SB = 'https://atheer-next-storybook.fly.dev/';

/** Status is carried by shape first, so it survives greyscale and small sizes. */
const shapes = [
  { key: 'square', label: 'Blocked' },
  { key: 'triangle', label: 'At risk' },
  { key: 'circle', label: 'On track' },
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title={storyTitle('/ops-manager')}
  metaTitle="Operational software — live product and component library | Tyler Young"
  description="A working field-operations surface: status carried by shape rather than colour, and a sidebar that surfaces what changed instead of waiting to be clicked."
  eyebrow={storyEyebrow('/ops-manager')}
  meta={[
    ['Live', `<a href="${APP}">atheer-next.fly.dev</a>`],
    ['Components', `<a href="${SB}">atheer-next-storybook.fly.dev</a>`],
    ['Persona', 'Monitors field work all day; arrives resuming, not browsing'],
    ['Built with', 'Paper↔Claude Code · React · Tailwind · Storybook'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    <Draft>
      The product and its component library are both running software, not
      screenshots — the two links in the margin. Below is what to look at once you
      are in them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Status uses shape, not just colour</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Open the product and look at the sidebar and the job IDs. Status is a hollow
      shape — square, triangle, circle — not a colour. The same shape appears on
      the nav item, the job header and the status column, so the blocked square in
      the sidebar is the same mark on the row that caused it.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      It works in greyscale, it works at 11px, and it frees colour to do something
      else.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <ul class="cells three shape-key">
    {
      shapes.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <svg width="34" height="34" viewBox="0 0 34 34" role="img" aria-label={`${s.label}: a ${s.key}`}>
            {s.key === 'square' && <rect x="6" y="6" width="22" height="22" class="glyph" />}
            {s.key === 'triangle' && <path d="M 17 5 L 29 28 L 5 28 Z" class="glyph" />}
            {s.key === 'circle' && <circle cx="17" cy="17" r="11" class="glyph" />}
          </svg>
          <span>{s.label}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>The sidebar carries live state</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Every item in the nav carries a live line underneath it: a certification
      expiring tomorrow, a safety step skipped on a job, a rework rate up this
      week. The nav stops being a menu and starts being today's problems. People
      find features by working, not by being trained.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <StoryFrame
    id="components-sidebar--home-active"
    label="Sidebar"
    source="Paper node IKC-1"
    height={712}
    note="Live from the component library. Configurable: active item, labels, subtitle text, status shapes, icons."
  />

  <h2>Every component traces to a design</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Every component traces back to a specific node in the design file. The
      inventory records which one, and what you can configure on each. Nothing was
      built on spec. If it never appeared in a design, it was not in the library.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <StoryFrame
    id="components-equipmentcard--default"
    label="EquipmentCard"
    source="Paper node IRG-1"
    height={690}
    note="Title, spec values, section labels and link lists are all configurable; variants cover many links, no image, and two theme palettes."
  />

  <p>
    <Draft>
      The library also carries the token page: colours, type, spacing, radii,
      borders, shadows. That is the constraint everything else is generated inside.
      Open it first if you want to know whether this was a real system or a folder
      of components.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <p class="artifact-row">
    <a class="artifact" href={SB} data-live target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Open the component library
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
    <a class="artifact" href={APP} data-live target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Open the product
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </p>


  <h2>What shipped</h2>
  <p>
    90 days produced a working pipeline and a v0 of the design system. The team
    carried it forward.
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      The data in the live product is invented — the equipment, the sites and the
      people are not real. A second surface from the same engagement carries a named
      end customer and is deliberately not published.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocumentLayout>

<style>

  .fig {
    margin: 0 0 34px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-paper-raised);
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .fig svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 372px;
    height: auto;
  }

  .glyph {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-ink);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }

  .glyph-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    fill: var(--color-ink-3);
    text-anchor: middle;
  }

  .artifact-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 14px 26px;
    margin: 30px 0 0 !important;
  }
</style>
